th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/integral-test-for-convergence.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/integral-test-for-convergence.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition is required for the function in the integral test?","options":["Nonnegative and monotonically increasing.","Nonnegative and monotonically decreasing.","Monotonically decreasing, of either sign.","Nonnegative and tending to $0$ at infinity."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eef9408db0d5b20914771347","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Why does $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^2}$ converge?","options":["Because $\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ converges","Because $\\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ diverges","Because the integral test gives its exact sum","Because $\\frac{1}{n^2}$ is negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e62b4cede67b44135c0c88de","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the integral test provide?","options":["The exact sum, equal to $\\int_1^{\\infty}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.","A convergence comparison, not the sum's value.","A convergence test for any series of positive terms.","A way to prove divergence only, never convergence."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac88f5608c70e898fab82760","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is monotone decrease important in the integral test?","options":["It guarantees that $f(x)\\to0$ as $x\\to\\infty$.","It allows comparison with rectangle areas.","It removes the need for nonnegativity.","It makes the improper integral and the sum equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"79294ccc27fec0b1e487c068","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\sin^2(\\pi x)$ on $[1,\\infty)$, which is continuous and nonnegative but not monotone. How do $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}f(n)$ and $\\int_1^{\\infty}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ behave?","options":["The series converges, but the integral diverges","Both converge, since $f(n)=0$ for every $n$","Both diverge, since $f(x)$ does not tend to $0$","The series diverges, but the integral converges"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdf546842e9cdad7b3e18948","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_010_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, the integral test says that $\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n\\frac{1}{i}-\\ln n\\right)_{n=1}^{\\infty}$ converges, to about $0.577$. What does this tell us about the harmonic series?","options":["Its partial sums grow like $\\ln n$","It converges, with sum about $0.577$","Its partial sums equal $\\ln n$ for large $n$","Its partial sums stay bounded"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b871b2240d80f427ef0f92ce","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_012_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Apply the integral test with $f(x)=1/x$ on $[1,\\infty)$ to the harmonic series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 1/n$. Which conclusion about convergence is correct?","options":["It diverges, because $\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ diverges","It converges, because its terms $1/n$ tend to $0$","It converges, because $\\int_1^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ is finite","The test does not apply, since $1/x$ is not defined at $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1395aaf20ada5e978a006686","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integral_test_for_convergence_5acd1c2e_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x^2}$ on $[1,\\infty)$, which is continuous, nonnegative and nonincreasing, so that $\\sum_{n=2}^{N}f(n)\\le\\int_1^N f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\le\\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}f(n)$ for every integer $N\\ge2$. Which bounds on $S=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n^2}$ follow from these inequalities?","options":["$0\\le S\\le1$","$S=1$","$1\\le S\\le2$","$S\\ge2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e349513bebbbd7d0a160e87","time_limit":30}]}}